{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And in all that I have said to you, you shall be watchful</b> — Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 23:13) explained: to perform every positive commandment by way of admonition, for every 'watching' (שְׁמִירָה) in the Torah constitutes an admonition in the place of a negative prohibition. According to his words, the Rav would need to say that this is a general prohibition (לָאו שֶׁבִּכְלָלוֹת), for if not, they would administer lashes for every positive commandment in the Torah. But in the case of this negative prohibition that generalizes many things without mentioning the name of the transgression at all, everyone agrees that one does not receive lashes for it. But they have already said: 'the watchful — for a positive commandment, perform the positive commandment' (Eruvin 96a), and if so, he added nothing here except a positive commandment. And in the Mekhilta (here) they disagreed concerning this verse and interpreted it in many ways. According to the plain sense, its meaning is: 'in all that I have said to you — concerning other elohim you shall be watchful,' for the verse is connected at its end, that is to say: from all the many admonitions that I have spoken to you, concerning other elohim you shall be greatly watchful — that you not serve them and not bow down to them, and that you put under ban those who slaughter to them, and that you not make a carved image or any likeness, and furthermore you shall be watchful not to mention the name of their divinity — to say 'Chemosh elohim of Moab,' 'Milcom elohim of the children of Ammon' (I Kings 11:33), 'Ashima elohim of Hamath' (II Kings 17:30). Let their name not be heard on your lips even without the epithet of divinity — to mention Milcom and Ashima at all; rather, you shall designate them pejoratively: 'the abomination of Moab' (I Kings 11:7), 'the detestation of the children of Ammon' (II Kings 23:13). Or, it shall not be heard in the mouth of its worshippers on your account — in the sense that our Rabbis said (Sanhedrin 63b): that one should not form a partnership with a gentile and thereby come to swear to him by his idol. <br><br>And it is possible that 'you shall not mention' (לֹא תַזְכִּירוּ) means: you shall not mention it to its worshippers, saying, 'By your elohim, do kindness with me,' and 'let no remembrance of it be heard on your lips' (לֹא יִשָּׁמַע עַל פִּיךָ זֵכֶר שְׁמוֹ כְּלָל) — meaning any mention of its name at all. And this is what is stated in the book of Joshua (23:7): 'and by the name of their elohim you shall not mention, and you shall not make them swear, and you shall not serve them.' There Scripture added to explain that the admonition is not to mention or make any person swear by the elohim of the nations."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes I Kings 11:33 and II Kings 17:30 using the forms 'מלכום' and 'אשימא'; I have rendered these as 'Milcom' and 'Ashima' following standard biblical English."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:14:55.351478214Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}